<ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Services</strong>: register each backend API once: its address, its endpoints and how the gateway signs in to it.</li>
    <li><strong>Endpoints</strong>: build an aggregated endpoint by picking service endpoints as steps. Apps call one address, for example <code>GET /bff/customer/C-10042/overview</code>, and get one combined answer.</li>
    <li><strong>Try it</strong>, then <strong>Publish</strong>. Apps can use it straight away; nothing needs redeploying.</li>
</ol>
<p><a class="button primary" href="/endpoints/new?guide=first">Build your first endpoint with the guide</a></p>
<section id="roles">
<h2>Roles <GuideLink Guide="getting-started#3-look-around-the-admin-ui-3-minutes" /></h2>
<p>You sign in to this admin app as yourself. Your name appears at the top right, with <strong>Sign out</strong> next to it. What you can do depends on your role:</p>
<table class="list">
    <thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Can</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>Author</td><td>Add services and their sign-ins, build endpoints, save drafts and use <strong>Try it</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Publisher</td><td>Everything an author can, plus <strong>Publish</strong>, <strong>Roll back to this</strong> and <strong>Delete endpoint</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Admin</td><td>A separate role for the platform team: add and remove the websites allowed to call the gateway from a browser (<strong>Settings</strong>). It doesn't let you build or publish endpoints; ask for author or publisher too if you need them</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
<ul>
    <li><strong>On your own computer (development)</strong>, the sign-in asks only for a name and a role (tick <strong>Also admin</strong> to try the Settings page). There's no password. Pick <strong>Author</strong> to see what someone who can't publish sees.</li>
    <li><strong>In test and production</strong>, you sign in with your organisation's account. Your role comes from there: ask your administrator if you need publisher.</li>
    <li>The history of each endpoint records who published it, with the name you signed in with.</li>
</ul>
<p class="hint">Signing in here is about <em>you</em>. A service's <strong>sign-in</strong> (on the Services page) is how the gateway proves who <em>it</em> is to that service. They're separate.</p>
</section>
